## What the scan pins down

One finding, `crime_00008` — **Sync I/O in Hot Path** (`sync_io_in_hotpath`, `src/api/files.ts:12–15`). Evidence verbatim:

> - `` `existsSync` @L12 in `loadPlanSchema` (domain function) ``
> - `` `statSync` @L13 in `loadPlanSchema` (domain function) ``
> - `` `readFileSync` @L15 in `loadPlanSchema` (domain function) ``
> - `lines: 12, 13, 15`
> - `` swap for the async variant (`readFile`, `writeFile`, `exec`, …) and `await` it ``

Summary line: *"3 synchronous I/O calls inside domain function — each invocation blocks the event loop for the duration of the I/O. Async counterparts exist for every method captured."*

## The three methods

`existsSync`, `statSync`, `readFileSync` — all from `node:fs`, all in `loadPlanSchema` (`src/api/files.ts:11-16`). That's **three blocking syscall round-trips per call**, not one: an access check, a stat, then a full file read that blocks until the last byte lands. Under concurrent load every one of those parks the single event-loop thread, so unrelated in-flight requests queue behind disk latency. That is exactly the shape of "p99 spikes, event loop lag reported, mean stays fine."

Note the detector scored this `severity: low` / `scores.severity: 0.4`. That's a static heuristic with no runtime traffic model — it can't see that this sits on your hottest path. The evidence is the load-bearing part, not the grade.

## Smallest async migration

Read the file. All three syscalls collapse into one `readFile`, because the read itself already tells you everything the first two were asking:

```ts
import { readFile } from "node:fs/promises";

export async function loadPlanSchema(): Promise<unknown> {
  let raw: string;
  try {
    raw = await readFile(SCHEMA_PATH, "utf8");
  } catch (err) {
    if ((err as NodeJS.ErrnoException).code === "ENOENT") return null;
    throw err;
  }
  if (raw.length === 0) return null;
  return JSON.parse(raw);
}
```

Behaviour preserved exactly: missing file → `null` (was `existsSync`), empty file → `null` (was `stat.size === 0`; 0 bytes ⟺ 0 chars, so the check is equivalent), otherwise the parsed JSON. Three syscalls become one, and the one that remains runs on the threadpool instead of the main thread. Free bonus: the `existsSync`-then-read TOCTOU race disappears.

**On "preserves the public signature":** the name, arity (zero params), export form and resolved value type are all unchanged — but the return type necessarily becomes `Promise<unknown>`. There is no way around that; a call that blocks cannot stop blocking while still handing back a value synchronously. Any "solution" that keeps `(): unknown` is caching or a sync-over-async shim, and both are the papering-over you ruled out.

Two things that follow from it:

- No in-repo callers exist (`grep` for `loadPlanSchema` across the fixture returns only the declaration), so the migration is contained to this file today.
- It's an exported symbol, so external callers must add `await`. Watch for the silent-failure case: a Promise is always truthy, so an existing `if (loadPlanSchema())` or `if (!loadPlanSchema())` guard will not throw — it will just quietly stop working. Grep call sites for bare truthiness checks rather than trusting the type error to catch them all.

Unrelated to the latency: the same file carries `crime_00016` (`` `localhost:3000` @L8 ``) and `crime_00017` (`` `/Users/andrew/dev/crimes/schema.json` @L9 ``). The hardcoded path is worth fixing in the same pass since you're already touching how the schema is located, but neither is causing the event loop lag.
